WEBVTT

00:13.620 --> 00:21.020
The Raptor engine, in my opinion, is one of the most impressive feats of engineering that's happened at SpaceX and maybe in the world.

00:23.780 --> 00:26.360
But it is a complex device.

00:28.440 --> 00:33.400
And with complexity, there come many, many ways for things to go wrong.

00:36.900 --> 00:43.400
And so relying on this engine to get us there for that final 1% of a historic return

00:43.400 --> 00:45.860
to launch site mission is pretty scary.

00:52.200 --> 00:55.240
T plus one minute, maintaining 33 engines.

01:04.160 --> 01:05.480
Six engines.

01:07.060 --> 01:08.520
Stage separation.

01:10.880 --> 01:13.080
31 engines for boostback.

01:21.540 --> 01:24.020
On Anomaly 2, acknowledging Engine 6.

01:33.880 --> 01:38.540
On Raptor 2, for awareness, it appears the E6 took an ORCU controller reset, which is

01:38.540 --> 01:39.340
what drove the shutdown.

01:40.180 --> 01:43.680
And on Prop 2, let's take a look at all of our systems, make sure we're good to go for

01:43.680 --> 01:44.520
orbit here.

01:48.200 --> 01:51.500
On Anomaly 2, with E6 shut down, we are not committing to orbit.

02:17.720 --> 02:22.740
The risks involved with our first orbital flight are largely centered around the demisability

02:22.740 --> 02:23.300
of the ship.

02:26.040 --> 02:31.580
We have a lot we got to get right to do this, especially when it comes to sustaining a healthy

02:31.580 --> 02:34.100
vehicle that can get out of orbit late in missions.

02:35.080 --> 02:38.600
In the space world, you either have rockets or you have spacecraft.

02:40.980 --> 02:44.560
Rockets are designed as the transportation system that gets you up.

02:45.180 --> 02:47.800
Spacecraft are things that live in orbit for a long time.

02:48.200 --> 02:52.800
We're trying to make something that's a rocket, but that can act like a spacecraft.

02:59.960 --> 03:03.280
This is a vehicle that can be in orbit for a long time,

03:03.280 --> 03:05.980
and after that can light the Raptor engines

03:05.980 --> 03:09.840
and come safely out of orbit and survive.

03:13.020 --> 03:17.900
This is one of two tiles that we took off of Ship 40.

03:19.320 --> 03:23.060
The goal here is to prove for the first time ever

03:23.060 --> 03:26.620
on a Starship vehicle that tiles can be reflown.

03:31.140 --> 03:37.840
If you asked me the day we flew 40, if we were going to fly to Australia, rip off tiles,

03:38.140 --> 03:43.880
and then refly them on 41, I would have bet you like a year's salary that wasn't going to happen.

03:45.860 --> 03:51.160
To be able to hold the tile that has actually flown and come home, it's the beginning of that

03:51.160 --> 03:51.920
next chapter.

04:28.020 --> 04:30.080
I'd say there's lots of anticipation for today.

04:30.280 --> 04:32.060
This has been the buildup for a long time.

04:32.300 --> 04:36.800
This is our first orbital mission, so first big objective here is we're delivering useful

04:36.800 --> 04:40.400
payloads to orbit, so these will be functional Starlinks that will actually serve the constellation.

04:40.840 --> 04:43.920
Really the whole orbital test flight campaign has been building up to this.

04:44.260 --> 04:45.240
Go for launch.

04:46.840 --> 04:47.600
Flight committed.

05:24.100 --> 05:26.320
On Anomaly 2, we just got confirmation

05:26.620 --> 05:30.140
that we are no-go for orbit with this E6 premature shutdown.

05:41.060 --> 05:45.200
We've said it multiple times, we are only going to go to orbit

05:45.200 --> 05:48.920
when we are extremely confident in the health of the vehicle.

05:49.680 --> 05:51.560
It sounds like they're not committing to orbit.

05:51.700 --> 05:51.920
Okay.

05:53.200 --> 05:55.800
If they don't commit to orbit, do we have a plan for what we're doing?

05:56.720 --> 05:59.880
I don't know if they will dispense the satellites.

06:01.040 --> 06:02.200
Yeah, I don't think they do.

06:03.460 --> 06:04.240
Oh, yeah.

06:05.200 --> 06:06.500
And that would be the end of the show.

06:07.940 --> 06:08.560
Got it.

06:09.500 --> 06:12.800
Neil messaged that, yeah, the flight rule is to not proceed to orbit.

06:12.880 --> 06:13.160
Okay.

06:14.980 --> 06:16.960
So everything looked great until Engine 6 went out.

06:17.440 --> 06:20.360
Everything pre-flight we had determined, let's do the conservative thing.

06:20.360 --> 06:23.180
If the vehicle is unhealthy, do not commit to go to orbit.

06:23.360 --> 06:25.760
And we would do a disposal into the Indian Ocean.

06:27.480 --> 06:30.060
The moment we lost the engine, we all kind of knew

06:30.060 --> 06:32.020
this was the end of the mission for the ship.

06:33.320 --> 06:35.940
There's only 17 minutes in that first coast to do anything.

06:37.120 --> 06:39.620
There's a pneumatics leak.

06:41.880 --> 06:44.380
This is why we need more engines on the ship.

06:44.400 --> 06:46.440
Let me do another scrub. It looks okay.

06:48.040 --> 06:50.820
There was lots of uncertainty. We had done the conservative thing.

06:51.000 --> 06:54.500
We had thought, like, hey, let's not proceed if anything goes wrong.

06:54.800 --> 06:56.480
And Elon really questioned the requirements,

06:56.620 --> 06:59.660
and asked us, is there anything actually prohibiting us from going forward?

07:00.320 --> 07:05.620
A development program has a very different risk concept to a production program.

07:06.960 --> 07:11.800
On launch day, we get risks from the engine team, from avionics, from the computers,

07:12.180 --> 07:14.320
from the propulsion system, from all the plumbing on the rocket.

07:14.520 --> 07:18.260
We get risks from the launch pad. We get risks from the tower and the catch arms.

07:18.940 --> 07:23.400
But the goal is always to take the risk in the place where you can learn the most.

07:23.600 --> 07:25.520
And that's the calculus that we're doing every day.

07:26.620 --> 07:28.380
So Jake was the final go for the engine systems

07:28.380 --> 07:30.360
and made the recommendation to Elon.

07:33.140 --> 07:35.440
We have exec override and concurrence.

07:35.520 --> 07:37.160
We want to go to orbit with this condition.

07:37.320 --> 07:38.260
We would like to lift the hold.

07:40.680 --> 07:41.840
I can weigh in here.

07:41.940 --> 07:43.780
They're saying that they're going to likely proceed

07:43.780 --> 07:45.800
with orbit pending a final go/no-go four minutes

07:45.800 --> 07:47.080
before the orbital insertion burn.

07:47.700 --> 07:48.340
Thanks, Thomas.

07:53.660 --> 07:57.480
This is the go/no-go poll for Burn 2 to orbit. Provide your status when polled.

07:57.740 --> 07:58.280
Stage 2.

07:58.520 --> 07:59.340
Stage 2's go.

07:59.520 --> 08:00.080
Raptor 2.

08:00.420 --> 08:01.740
Raptor 2's go for orbit.

08:02.680 --> 08:03.480
Burn 2 startup.

08:08.940 --> 08:09.800
Early coast.

08:15.920 --> 08:17.080
Burn 2 shutdown.

08:24.620 --> 08:26.200
No alerts or alarms on the board.

08:34.600 --> 08:35.460
We're in orbit.

08:44.780 --> 08:45.400
All right.

08:47.340 --> 08:48.420
Yeah, well, it looks good.

08:50.080 --> 08:51.080
Now for the fun part.

09:17.160 --> 09:19.060
Yeah, I can't say I slept a ton last night.

09:19.240 --> 09:23.120
Just, like, really quite excited to get these satellites into orbit.

09:24.440 --> 09:25.240
First contact.

09:25.880 --> 09:29.280
You can't overstate how important of a milestone this is for Starship.

09:29.440 --> 09:31.600
I mean, the team has been working on this for so long,

09:31.700 --> 09:35.280
and this rocket is obviously going to be transformational for spaceflight,

09:35.460 --> 09:36.120
for humanity.

09:36.780 --> 09:38.040
All sats in contact.

09:41.360 --> 09:42.900
Shout out to the Starship team.

09:43.200 --> 09:44.820
Gave us a little heart attack there,

09:44.960 --> 09:47.180
whether they were going orbital or they weren't going orbital.

09:47.460 --> 09:50.340
But real happy they ended up getting our sats up there.

09:50.600 --> 09:53.440
And yeah, all 26 sats connected to the network.

09:55.860 --> 09:59.540
In Redmond and in Starbase, we're two separate teams.

09:59.600 --> 10:01.420
But we do partner together.

10:01.420 --> 10:05.400
And it's so exciting to collaborate and to be able to be

10:05.460 --> 10:08.280
their payload and have a successful mission.

10:08.560 --> 10:13.260
It's not just a test ship now, it's a true mission.

10:21.960 --> 10:26.040
This is the thing that proves out that, yeah, this rocket has a useful purpose.

10:26.260 --> 10:29.820
And as it starts adding satellites to the Starlink constellation, it's going to make

10:29.820 --> 10:31.160
our network better, our service better.

10:31.160 --> 10:34.840
And then it's going to roll that all into continuing to build the flywheel

10:34.840 --> 10:38.960
of making Starship continuously launch, continuously be reused.

10:58.260 --> 11:01.560
All things considered, it's been basically a spotless mission so far.

11:01.820 --> 11:07.440
All 26 satellites deployed, and they've been successfully climbing ever since then.

11:09.720 --> 11:15.420
At Starlink, we think fundamentally the technology is able to support on the order of half of

11:15.420 --> 11:17.340
all the internet traffic that is on Earth today.

11:17.740 --> 11:20.940
And so really the challenge is on us to go and deliver on that goal.

11:55.880 --> 12:02.200
On a per satellite basis, these vehicles are basically 10 times as performance as our V2 satellites.

12:03.420 --> 12:07.660
If you were to just swap out our entire V2 fleet with V3s,

12:07.720 --> 12:09.900
you would have a network that was 10 times as capable.

12:10.460 --> 12:12.860
And so once Starship gets to like a monthly cadence

12:12.860 --> 12:15.600
with like 12 launches a year, that's essentially tying

12:15.600 --> 12:18.660
like our best year on Starlink in terms of like network growth.

12:19.600 --> 12:23.160
So it's really a turning point where we shift from development mode

12:23.160 --> 12:24.440
into production mode.

12:24.640 --> 12:27.720
Every launch from here going forward is going to generate real value.

12:41.920 --> 12:46.640
We have like 30 minutes to make a call, like right after payload deploy.

12:47.840 --> 12:52.640
So we had two planned landing locations for this mission, one in Chile with a nine-hour

12:52.640 --> 12:55.660
mission and a backup landing site at Hawaii.

12:56.180 --> 13:00.120
The reason we did two landing sites was so that if we had any issues arise,

13:00.320 --> 13:04.220
we had outs along the way that allowed us to put the rocket down somewhere safe.

13:05.620 --> 13:07.880
It's like 90% of the shit that we want.

13:08.120 --> 13:09.880
What are you most worried about?

13:11.000 --> 13:14.500
Engine shutdown is an elevated risk,

13:14.880 --> 13:17.000
and then helium is somewhat of an elevated risk.

13:17.000 --> 13:18.750
You know, with the settling.

13:19.250 --> 13:23.120
And then the reentry is a higher risk.

13:23.880 --> 13:26.500
Hawaii has a really good runway.

13:27.120 --> 13:29.000
It's just a low risk reentry.

13:32.200 --> 13:34.240
The real risk is are we fucking up and missing something?

13:34.480 --> 13:37.040
Because we're not looking at the right thing right now.

13:39.900 --> 13:41.980
So it seems like they're chatting it.

13:45.020 --> 13:48.260
Starship's coasts are going to be quite long for a orbital stage.

13:48.520 --> 13:51.420
It's the longest that any vehicle of its caliber and size

13:51.420 --> 13:53.320
will ever have coasted and then tried to relight.

13:57.140 --> 13:59.880
Yeah, one of the things that makes Starship particularly tricky

13:59.880 --> 14:03.240
towards the long duration on orbit is the cryogenic propellants.

14:03.500 --> 14:06.720
And over time, you have lots of heat from your environment

14:06.720 --> 14:09.100
that basically wants to boil that propellant,

14:09.180 --> 14:10.540
kind of like boiling water on a stove.

14:14.940 --> 14:18.380
And so how do we keep the liquid at a happy temperature,

14:18.460 --> 14:21.340
such that we have liquid remaining at the end of the mission

14:21.340 --> 14:22.800
to do the deorbit and landing burn?

14:23.680 --> 14:25.780
I agree with the decision to go to early Hawaii.

14:26.080 --> 14:29.220
I don't think that I would gate that just on using helium in the header tanks.

14:31.060 --> 14:32.120
Yeah, totally agree on that.

14:32.440 --> 14:35.480
Yeah, we're just chatting it here with Mark and Jake and E.

14:41.380 --> 14:44.000
E also wants to go to Hawaii.

14:47.100 --> 14:48.460
Confirming we're going to commit to Hawaii,

14:48.640 --> 14:50.720
given everything that's happened this far.

14:51.120 --> 14:53.120
We're going to Hawaii.

14:54.300 --> 14:57.160
We could get 99% of the way through

14:57.160 --> 14:59.860
a historic second stage return to launch site,

14:59.860 --> 15:02.100
but we need those Raptor engines to work.

15:02.320 --> 15:05.820
And that's why we put so much time into testing the engines

15:05.820 --> 15:08.300
to make sure they will work robustly.

15:44.120 --> 15:45.960
We are at Raptor Stand Bravo.

15:50.680 --> 15:54.500
We have two booster modules at this test position.

15:54.780 --> 15:58.400
And so basically what we did is we took a slice of the aft end of the vehicle

15:58.400 --> 16:00.400
and we put it right here in the bay,

16:00.920 --> 16:04.220
and we install it just like we do on the vehicle.

16:04.440 --> 16:07.380
And then we also test it just like we do on the vehicle.

16:10.560 --> 16:15.560
We first started testing Raptor engines, the very first 100-ton, horizontally,

16:15.680 --> 16:18.660
because we didn't want to build the infrastructure of a flame trench

16:18.660 --> 16:23.600
and the cost of digging down in a flame converter on something we didn't know was going to work.

16:23.800 --> 16:26.180
We had no idea if full-flow staged combustion was going to work.

16:26.180 --> 16:31.960
We did cold flow after cold flow after spin primes, just trying to figure out how to light it.

16:32.320 --> 16:34.900
Elon came to town and we lit it that evening.

16:37.880 --> 16:42.180
And then we went from 100 ton to V1 and then V2.

16:47.560 --> 16:49.140
And then started V3.

16:54.980 --> 16:59.780
Raptor is a full-flow staged combustion engine. That means that basically we burn all of the

16:59.780 --> 17:05.200
propellant and extract work from it in a turbine in each side of the engine before it goes into

17:05.200 --> 17:09.600
the main combustion chamber and gets burned again. One of the craziest things about Raptor is just

17:09.600 --> 17:14.380
the extreme temperature differences. In some of our test fire videos, especially McGregor,

17:14.380 --> 17:15.980
where you can get a really good view,

17:16.140 --> 17:18.920
you can actually see frost on the thrust chamber.

17:19.380 --> 17:20.780
And it's a little unintuitive

17:20.780 --> 17:23.080
because the thrust chamber is extremely hot,

17:23.200 --> 17:25.160
half the surface temperature of the sun on the inside,

17:25.380 --> 17:27.300
but on the outside, it's actually ice cold.

17:28.540 --> 17:30.240
That huge temperature delta

17:30.240 --> 17:31.960
is actually what gives us a lot of performance.

17:32.160 --> 17:33.440
We're extracting as much energy

17:33.440 --> 17:34.560
out of the propellants as possible.

17:35.000 --> 17:40.900
T minus five, four, three, two, one, zero.

17:53.200 --> 17:56.320
We want to make sure that we can get Starship out of space.

17:56.820 --> 18:00.140
And so we are primarily focused on deorbit testing.

18:00.500 --> 18:05.840
Everything is set so that you are testing the exact conditions that we will have in space

18:05.840 --> 18:09.680
and that we successfully light the engine when we do our deorbit burn.

18:12.580 --> 18:16.980
All right, ship is committed to early Hawaii contingency landing.

18:21.380 --> 18:26.860
We've been building towards this moment for a really long time, and circ burn was a very

18:26.860 --> 18:31.780
energizing and exciting moment because it worked and we were going to orbit, but none of that

18:31.780 --> 18:37.480
mattered if you can't get it back. So the worst case scenario is that we'll stay in orbit.

18:37.480 --> 18:40.740
Other vehicles are largely demisable when they come back and reenter, so they'll burn

18:40.740 --> 18:41.620
up in the atmosphere.

18:47.140 --> 18:52.060
Starship is obviously meant to be a reusable launch vehicle and is meant to survive atmospheric

18:52.060 --> 18:56.880
reentry, and so it's really important that we do not leave the vehicle on orbit in the

18:56.880 --> 19:02.140
event that it randomly reenters and comes back over land masses or populated areas.

19:02.600 --> 19:07.400
You do all this work, stand up all these contingencies, you get everything ready so that it does work,

19:07.560 --> 19:10.180
there's always a thought of like, what if it doesn't?

19:14.640 --> 19:15.700
Deorbit burn startup.

19:29.800 --> 19:30.920
Deorbit burn shutdown.

19:42.840 --> 19:43.560
Fuck yeah.

19:45.020 --> 19:45.160
Whoo!

19:53.380 --> 19:54.580
We're coming home.

19:56.980 --> 19:57.940
We did it.

20:01.140 --> 20:02.860
SpaceX as a whole has come down

20:02.860 --> 20:05.340
to a handful of unique decision points.

20:13.020 --> 20:16.940
When you get your smart people in a room using all the information ahead of them to make

20:16.940 --> 20:21.260
educated risk calls where it's go for broke, those calls are kind of the defining moments

20:21.260 --> 20:21.800
for the company.

20:23.360 --> 20:27.920
And in this case, it became the defining moment for Flight 14 and turning Starship into an

20:27.920 --> 20:28.680
orbital launch vehicle.

20:33.520 --> 20:36.380
This is a rehearsal poll for ship RTLS.

20:37.240 --> 20:42.300
Go indicates that the vehicle would continue to return to launch site had this been an RTLS mission.

20:44.100 --> 20:46.620
Provide your go/no-go status when polled.

20:49.240 --> 20:52.740
Structures? Go. Aero? Aero is go.

20:53.340 --> 20:58.600
Thank you. If this had been an RTLS mission, the vehicle would continue to return to launch site.
